 "post": {
  "title": "3 Facts About First Impressions #shorts",
  "description": "Rosa rehearsed 40 answers for her interview. Three facts she should know: 1) In a Princeton study (Willis & Todorov, 2006), people's judgments of a face after a 100-millisecond glimpse matched their judgments with no time limit — trustworthiness most of all. 2) Longer looks barely changed those judgments; they mostly made people more confident. 3) The halo effect (Nisbett & Wilson, 1977): students who watched the same lecturer act warm rather than cold rated his appearance, mannerisms and accent far more appealing, without realising his warmth had swayed them. #psychology #shorts #firstimpressions #interview",
